# Copy this file to .env (same folder as docker-compose.yml) before starting Docker,
# fill in your mail server, REMOVE the # at the start of each setting line, then run:  docker compose up -d
# .env is ignored by Git, so the password is never committed.
# Keep a .env file even if every line stays commented out: docker-compose.yml mounts it for AI drafting.
# While no mail settings are active, every email goes to the test inbox at http://localhost:8026

# Testing only: a Gmail mailbox (needs 2-Step Verification and an App Password). Production uses Outlook below.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_TLS=true
# SMTP_USER=your.address@gmail.com
# SMTP_PASSWORD=your-16-letter-app-password
# MAIL_FROM=your.address@gmail.com

# Your company name, used in email wording and AI email drafts
# COMPANY_NAME=SRS Consulting

# Every email goes out FROM this one address (shown as "<user name> via SalesTrack");
# customer replies go to the salesperson's own email (Reply-To).

# ---------- Outlook, option A (for now): a mailbox's own login over SMTP ----------
# Works only if SMTP AUTH is allowed for that mailbox (IT can turn it on) and the account has no MFA prompt
# (or use an app password). Microsoft turns this off by default for existing tenants in late December 2026.
# Limits: 30 emails a minute, 10,000 recipients a day, 2,000 EXTERNAL recipients a day per mailbox.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.office365.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_TLS=true
# SMTP_USER=your.name@srsconsultinginc.com
# SMTP_PASSWORD=your-password
# MAIL_FROM=your.name@srsconsultinginc.com

# ---------- Outlook, option B (recommended for the server): Microsoft 365 with OAuth (Graph) ----------
# IT registers an app in Azure (Entra ID) with the Microsoft Graph "Mail.Send" application permission
# (admin consent), ideally limited to the CRM mailbox, and gives you these three values. No password is stored.
# MAIL_PROVIDER=graph
# MS_TENANT_ID=your-tenant-id
# MS_CLIENT_ID=your-app-client-id
# MS_CLIENT_SECRET=your-app-secret
# MAIL_FROM=crm@yourcompany.com

# Check it at http://localhost:8001/docs (log in as Admin): GET /api/health/email, then POST /api/health/email/test

# ---------- AI (AI Assistant, record summaries and AI email drafts) ----------
# The AI features are off until an OpenAI API key is set here. Restart with: docker compose up -d
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1   # optional: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint

# ---------- LinkedIn autofill on New Lead (Apollo, optional) ----------
# Pasting a LinkedIn profile address in New Lead fills the details Apollo returns. Off while this is empty.
# Create the key in Apollo (https://docs.apollo.io/docs/create-api-key) with access to api/v1/people/match.
# It stays on the backend. Each matched lookup can use Apollo credits (no match = no credit); personal emails and
# mobile numbers are never requested. Restart with: docker compose up -d
APOLLO_API_KEY=

# ---------- Email campaigns ----------
# Campaign emails go out a few at a time from the MAIL_FROM mailbox, under Outlook's limits
# (30 a minute, 10,000 recipients a day, 2,000 EXTERNAL recipients a day from Oct 2026).
# CAMPAIGN_PER_MINUTE=20
# CAMPAIGN_PER_DAY=1800
# Every campaign email ends with Unsubscribe and Subscribe links. They must open from the lead's inbox, so set the address
# where SalesTrack is reachable from outside (e.g. https://crm.yourcompany.com). Empty = APP_URL (localhost: test only).
# PUBLIC_URL=https://crm.yourcompany.com

# ---------- Login ----------
# On the first start the CRM creates this Admin (only if there is no active Admin yet).
# Log in with it, change the password (profile menu > Change Password), then add users on the Users page.
# ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@salestrack.local
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=Admin@2026
# Email domains allowed for new users (comma separated). Personal emails like Gmail are refused.
# Leave it empty (ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=) to allow any domain.
# ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=srsconsultinginc.com
# Signs the login tokens. Put any long random text here (anyone who knows it could create logins).
# SECRET_KEY=change-this-to-a-long-random-text

# ---------- Server (deployment): see DEPLOY.md ----------
# APP_URL=https://crm.yourcompany.com      # the address people open; used in notification email links
# APP_PORT=80                              # port the app listens on (default 5173)
# API_PORT=127.0.0.1:8001                  # API docs only from the server itself
# DB_PORT=127.0.0.1:5433                   # database only from the server itself
# MAILPIT_PORT=127.0.0.1:8026              # test inbox only from the server itself

# ---------- Optional: one database shared by several computers ----------
# Every login on this computer already shares one database (the "db" container).
# To have teammates' computers use the same data, point them all at one PostgreSQL server:
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://crm_user:crm_pass@192.168.1.20:5433/crm_db
# APP_URL=http://192.168.1.20:5173        # link used in notification emails

# ---------- AI email drafting (optional, backend only) ----------
# Generate subject/message drafts in the email composer. Sending remains a separate action.
# Use sample for local stage-based examples without a key (not AI generation).
# EMAIL_DRAFT_MODE=sample
# Use openai for real AI drafting with the key below.
# EMAIL_DRAFT_MODE=openai
# OPENAI_API_KEY=your-openai-api-key
# OPENAI_EMAIL_MODEL=gpt-4.1-mini
# After setting the key in .env, run: docker compose up -d --build

# Docker reads OPENAI_API_KEY from this file through a read-only secret mount.
# The key is not copied to frontend code, images, or Docker environment configuration.

# ---------- Local AI email drafting with Ollama (no OpenAI key required) ----------
# EMAIL_DRAFT_MODE=ollama
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434  # Docker Desktop to your Mac
# OLLAMA_EMAIL_MODEL=qwen3.5:9b  # must match ollama list
# OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=30m  # keep the model loaded between drafts to avoid repeated loading
# When running the backend outside Docker, use http://localhost:11434 instead.
